1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,912
Sweetheart, when I turned seventy-two,

2
00:00:02,912 --> 00:00:05,365
my doctor told me my hearing was

3
00:00:05,365 --> 00:00:05,979
perfect.

4
00:00:06,159 --> 00:00:08,261
But I came home and told my

5
00:00:08,261 --> 00:00:10,129
family I was going deaf.

6
00:00:10,309 --> 00:00:12,967
I bought a hearing aid that didn't

7
00:00:12,967 --> 00:00:15,469
work, and I wore it to Christmas

8
00:00:15,469 --> 00:00:16,954
dinner, and I said,

9
00:00:16,954 --> 00:00:17,345
what?

10
00:00:17,525 --> 00:00:18,651
to everything.

11
00:00:18,831 --> 00:00:21,114
And for one whole Christmas,

12
00:00:21,114 --> 00:00:23,886
my family talked right in front of

13
00:00:23,886 --> 00:00:25,925
me, as if I wasn't there.

14
00:00:26,105 --> 00:00:28,436
What I heard changed my will,

15
00:00:28,436 --> 00:00:30,284
my house, and my heart.

16
00:00:30,635 --> 00:00:32,860
Tonight I'm going to tell you why

17
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:33,467
I did it,

18
00:00:33,467 --> 00:00:35,625
the three things I heard at that

19
00:00:35,625 --> 00:00:38,053
table, and the one person who didn't

20
00:00:38,053 --> 00:00:40,279
change at all when they thought I

21
00:00:40,279 --> 00:00:41,223
couldn't hear.

22
00:00:41,403 --> 00:00:42,557
Stay with me,

23
00:00:42,557 --> 00:00:46,018
honey, because I was wrong about almost

24
00:00:46,018 --> 00:00:46,906
everybody.

25
00:00:47,086 --> 00:00:49,216
And the person I was most wrong

26
00:00:49,216 --> 00:00:51,689
about is the reason I'm telling this

27
00:00:51,689 --> 00:00:52,101
story.

28
00:00:52,452 --> 00:00:55,462
It started with something my late husband

29
00:00:55,462 --> 00:00:57,664
said, the year before he died.

30
00:00:57,844 --> 00:00:59,437
Harold said, Ruthie,

31
00:00:59,437 --> 00:01:02,543
people don't change when you can't hear

32
00:01:02,543 --> 00:01:02,941
them.

33
00:01:03,121 --> 00:01:05,281
They just stop pretending.

34
00:01:05,461 --> 00:01:07,761
He'd lost most of his hearing in

35
00:01:07,761 --> 00:01:09,845
the plant, and he told me the

36
00:01:09,845 --> 00:01:12,361
things people said in front of him,

37
00:01:12,361 --> 00:01:14,230
thinking he couldn't hear,

38
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:15,596
would curl my hair.

39
00:01:15,776 --> 00:01:17,704
Some of them were about me,

40
00:01:17,704 --> 00:01:18,133
honey.

41
00:01:18,313 --> 00:01:19,857
Kind things.

42
00:01:20,037 --> 00:01:21,349
And some weren't.

43
00:01:21,699 --> 00:01:23,446
After Harold passed,

44
00:01:23,446 --> 00:01:24,844
I had the house,

45
00:01:24,844 --> 00:01:26,330
a little savings,

46
00:01:26,330 --> 00:01:28,339
and five grandchildren.

47
00:01:28,519 --> 00:01:31,132
And everybody was so sweet to me,

48
00:01:31,132 --> 00:01:32,002
sweetheart.

49
00:01:32,182 --> 00:01:34,342
Phone calls on Sundays.

50
00:01:34,522 --> 00:01:36,519
Flowers on my birthday.

51
00:01:36,699 --> 00:01:39,636
But I'd started to notice little things.

52
00:01:39,816 --> 00:01:42,581
A look between my daughter-in-law and my

53
00:01:42,581 --> 00:01:42,858
son.

54
00:01:43,038 --> 00:01:45,784
The way conversations stopped when I walked

55
00:01:45,784 --> 00:01:46,869
into the kitchen.

56
00:01:47,049 --> 00:01:49,126
And I wanted to know the truth

57
00:01:49,126 --> 00:01:51,133
while I was still alive to do

58
00:01:51,133 --> 00:01:52,448
something about it.

59
00:01:52,798 --> 00:01:53,945
So in November,

60
00:01:53,945 --> 00:01:56,622
I told everybody the doctor said my

61
00:01:56,622 --> 00:01:57,999
hearing was going.

62
00:01:58,179 --> 00:02:00,224
I ordered a cheap hearing aid off

63
00:02:00,224 --> 00:02:02,456
the television for nineteen dollars,

64
00:02:02,456 --> 00:02:04,501
the kind that just makes a little

65
00:02:04,501 --> 00:02:05,493
whistling noise.

66
00:02:05,673 --> 00:02:07,577
I practiced saying,

67
00:02:07,577 --> 00:02:08,680
what, dear?

68
00:02:08,860 --> 00:02:10,485
in front of the mirror.

69
00:02:10,665 --> 00:02:13,104
And I started tilting my head and

70
00:02:13,104 --> 00:02:13,916
leaning in.

71
00:02:14,096 --> 00:02:16,047
In a minute I'll tell you who

72
00:02:16,047 --> 00:02:18,671
saw through it in about thirty seconds.

73
00:02:18,851 --> 00:02:20,789
It wasn't who you'd think.

74
00:02:21,139 --> 00:02:23,553
My friend Dottie told me I was

75
00:02:23,553 --> 00:02:24,599
crazy, honey.

76
00:02:24,779 --> 00:02:25,921
She said, Ruth,

77
00:02:25,921 --> 00:02:29,040
you'll hear things you can never un-hear.

78
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:31,429
And some of them won't even be

79
00:02:31,429 --> 00:02:31,798
true.

80
00:02:31,978 --> 00:02:35,327
People say terrible things when they're tired

81
00:02:35,327 --> 00:02:36,146
and scared.

82
00:02:36,326 --> 00:02:37,775
I said, Dottie,

83
00:02:37,775 --> 00:02:39,321
I'm seventy-two.

84
00:02:39,501 --> 00:02:42,020
I don't have time to wonder anymore.

85
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:43,559
I'd rather know.

86
00:02:43,739 --> 00:02:45,936
She shook her head and said,

87
00:02:45,936 --> 00:02:47,349
then God help you.

88
00:02:47,529 --> 00:02:49,422
And she was right to worry.

89
00:02:49,602 --> 00:02:51,169
She usually was.

90
00:02:51,519 --> 00:02:53,000
On Christmas Day,

91
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:54,480
all of them came.

92
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:57,272
My son Mark and his wife,

93
00:02:57,272 --> 00:02:58,004
Denise.

94
00:02:58,184 --> 00:03:01,217
My daughter Susan and her husband,

95
00:03:01,217 --> 00:03:01,574
Tom.

96
00:03:01,754 --> 00:03:05,306
And five grandchildren from nine to twenty-three.

97
00:03:05,486 --> 00:03:07,795
Fourteen of us around my table,

98
00:03:07,795 --> 00:03:10,402
the way it had been every Christmas

99
00:03:10,402 --> 00:03:11,593
for fifty years.

100
00:03:11,773 --> 00:03:13,067
And at first,

101
00:03:13,067 --> 00:03:16,150
honey, everybody shouted at me.

102
00:03:16,330 --> 00:03:18,374
MERRY CHRISTMAS, MOM!

103
00:03:18,554 --> 00:03:20,318
DO YOU WANT MORE GRAVY?

104
00:03:20,498 --> 00:03:22,948
I just smiled and nodded.

105
00:03:23,298 --> 00:03:25,237
But by the second helping,

106
00:03:25,237 --> 00:03:26,804
they forgot about me.

107
00:03:26,984 --> 00:03:29,493
They started talking to each other across

108
00:03:29,493 --> 00:03:30,962
the table as if I were a

109
00:03:30,962 --> 00:03:31,268
lamp.

110
00:03:31,448 --> 00:03:33,697
And the first thing I heard,

111
00:03:33,697 --> 00:03:35,545
sweetheart, was Denise,

112
00:03:35,545 --> 00:03:36,670
my son's wife,

113
00:03:36,670 --> 00:03:38,437
leaning over to Susan.

114
00:03:38,617 --> 00:03:40,903
She said, have you seen the price

115
00:03:40,903 --> 00:03:42,634
of houses on this street?

116
00:03:42,814 --> 00:03:44,910
This place has to be worth three

117
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:46,285
hundred thousand now.

118
00:03:46,465 --> 00:03:47,728
And Susan said,

119
00:03:47,728 --> 00:03:50,506
Mark already talked to a realtor.

120
00:03:50,686 --> 00:03:52,079
Didn't he tell you?

121
00:03:52,429 --> 00:03:55,088
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

122
00:03:55,088 --> 00:03:57,139
tap the like button for me,

123
00:03:57,139 --> 00:03:57,595
honey.

124
00:03:57,775 --> 00:04:00,863
It helps more people find these stories.

125
00:04:01,043 --> 00:04:03,756
The second thing I heard was about

126
00:04:03,756 --> 00:04:04,793
where I'd go.

127
00:04:04,973 --> 00:04:06,227
My son Mark said,

128
00:04:06,227 --> 00:04:08,660
there's a nice place out on Route

129
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:09,176
Thirty.

130
00:04:09,356 --> 00:04:10,714
Sunny Acres.

131
00:04:10,894 --> 00:04:13,944
Mom wouldn't even notice the difference,

132
00:04:13,944 --> 00:04:15,469
the way she's going.

133
00:04:15,649 --> 00:04:16,673
And Tom said,

134
00:04:16,673 --> 00:04:19,747
does she have long-term care insurance?

135
00:04:19,927 --> 00:04:20,997
Because if not,

136
00:04:20,997 --> 00:04:23,707
that'll eat through the house money in

137
00:04:23,707 --> 00:04:24,420
two years.

138
00:04:24,771 --> 00:04:25,698
I sat there,

139
00:04:25,698 --> 00:04:28,018
sweetheart, buttering my roll,

140
00:04:28,018 --> 00:04:30,415
my heart pounding so hard I was

141
00:04:30,415 --> 00:04:32,271
sure they could hear it.

142
00:04:32,451 --> 00:04:33,693
My own son.

143
00:04:33,873 --> 00:04:36,227
The boy I'd rocked through croup,

144
00:04:36,227 --> 00:04:39,009
and driven to football practice at five

145
00:04:39,009 --> 00:04:40,079
in the morning,

146
00:04:40,079 --> 00:04:42,361
and paid for college with my egg

147
00:04:42,361 --> 00:04:42,789
money.

148
00:04:42,969 --> 00:04:45,504
Talking about my house like it was

149
00:04:45,504 --> 00:04:46,325
a used car.

150
00:04:46,505 --> 00:04:48,014
I kept smiling.

151
00:04:48,194 --> 00:04:49,862
I said, what,

152
00:04:49,862 --> 00:04:50,504
dear?

153
00:04:50,684 --> 00:04:53,124
when Susan asked me to pass the

154
00:04:53,124 --> 00:04:53,517
salt.

155
00:04:53,697 --> 00:04:55,044
And she laughed.

156
00:04:55,394 --> 00:04:57,787
And the third thing I heard,

157
00:04:57,787 --> 00:04:58,984
the worst one,

158
00:04:58,984 --> 00:05:00,950
was my oldest grandson,

159
00:05:00,950 --> 00:05:04,882
Kyle, twenty-three, who I'd always thought was

160
00:05:04,882 --> 00:05:05,993
my sweet boy.

161
00:05:06,173 --> 00:05:07,602
He was on his phone,

162
00:05:07,602 --> 00:05:09,458
and he said to his cousin,

163
00:05:09,458 --> 00:05:11,386
Grandma's getting so weird.

164
00:05:11,566 --> 00:05:13,354
I hate coming here.

165
00:05:13,534 --> 00:05:16,042
It smells like old people.

166
00:05:16,222 --> 00:05:18,942
I only came because Mom said she'd

167
00:05:18,942 --> 00:05:20,622
pay my car insurance.

168
00:05:20,802 --> 00:05:22,300
And they both laughed.

169
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:24,875
And something in me cracked right down

170
00:05:24,875 --> 00:05:25,568
the middle.

171
00:05:25,919 --> 00:05:27,621
And the strangest part,

172
00:05:27,621 --> 00:05:30,655
sweetheart, was how normal it all sounded

173
00:05:30,655 --> 00:05:31,247
to them.

174
00:05:31,427 --> 00:05:32,890
Nobody whispered.

175
00:05:33,070 --> 00:05:35,392
Nobody lowered their voice.

176
00:05:35,572 --> 00:05:37,750
They talked about my house and my

177
00:05:37,750 --> 00:05:39,862
money and my smell right over my

178
00:05:39,862 --> 00:05:42,105
head, while they ate my turkey and

179
00:05:42,105 --> 00:05:44,349
my stuffing and my mother's rolls,

180
00:05:44,349 --> 00:05:46,527
the same way you'd talk about the

181
00:05:46,527 --> 00:05:47,055
weather.

182
00:05:47,235 --> 00:05:49,069
That's what hurt the most.

183
00:05:49,249 --> 00:05:50,526
Not that they said it.

184
00:05:50,706 --> 00:05:52,506
That it was so easy.

185
00:05:52,856 --> 00:05:54,656
So far, the house,

186
00:05:54,656 --> 00:05:56,356
the nursing home,

187
00:05:56,356 --> 00:05:57,256
and Kyle.

188
00:05:57,436 --> 00:05:59,049
And then there was Lily.

189
00:05:59,229 --> 00:06:01,763
Lily was my youngest granddaughter,

190
00:06:01,763 --> 00:06:03,862
Susan's girl, nine years old.

191
00:06:04,042 --> 00:06:06,137
Quiet, funny little thing,

192
00:06:06,137 --> 00:06:08,071
with a gap in her teeth.

193
00:06:08,251 --> 00:06:10,238
She was sitting next to me,

194
00:06:10,238 --> 00:06:12,225
and halfway through dinner,

195
00:06:12,225 --> 00:06:14,580
she leaned over and looked at my

196
00:06:14,580 --> 00:06:16,494
hearing aid, very closely.

197
00:06:16,674 --> 00:06:18,833
Then she looked at my face.

198
00:06:19,013 --> 00:06:21,005
And then she whispered,

199
00:06:21,005 --> 00:06:23,950
so quietly nobody else could hear,

200
00:06:23,950 --> 00:06:26,548
Grandma, that's not turned on.

201
00:06:26,898 --> 00:06:28,460
I looked at her,

202
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:29,045
honey.

203
00:06:29,225 --> 00:06:30,816
And she looked at me.

204
00:06:30,996 --> 00:06:32,331
And she smiled,

205
00:06:32,331 --> 00:06:34,378
that gap-toothed smile,

206
00:06:34,378 --> 00:06:35,980
and she whispered,

207
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:37,138
I won't tell.

208
00:06:37,318 --> 00:06:39,399
And then for the rest of that

209
00:06:39,399 --> 00:06:42,989
dinner, every time somebody said something unkind,

210
00:06:42,989 --> 00:06:45,573
Lily would reach under the table and

211
00:06:45,573 --> 00:06:46,722
squeeze my hand.

212
00:06:46,902 --> 00:06:48,492
Every single time.

213
00:06:48,672 --> 00:06:49,868
Nine years old.

214
00:06:50,048 --> 00:06:52,335
She knew exactly what I was hearing,

215
00:06:52,335 --> 00:06:54,304
and she sat there holding on to

216
00:06:54,304 --> 00:06:54,495
me.

217
00:06:54,844 --> 00:06:58,166
After dinner, while the others watched football,

218
00:06:58,166 --> 00:07:00,173
Lily came and found me in the

219
00:07:00,173 --> 00:07:01,903
kitchen doing the dishes.

220
00:07:02,083 --> 00:07:04,627
She picked up a towel and started

221
00:07:04,627 --> 00:07:06,785
drying, without being asked.

222
00:07:06,965 --> 00:07:08,072
And she said,

223
00:07:08,072 --> 00:07:10,797
Grandma, why are you pretending?

224
00:07:10,977 --> 00:07:13,057
And I told her the truth,

225
00:07:13,057 --> 00:07:13,972
sweetheart.

226
00:07:14,152 --> 00:07:16,457
I said, because I wanted to know

227
00:07:16,457 --> 00:07:18,763
who loves me when they think I'm

228
00:07:18,763 --> 00:07:19,771
not listening.

229
00:07:19,951 --> 00:07:21,304
And she said,

230
00:07:21,304 --> 00:07:22,552
oh, Grandma.

231
00:07:22,732 --> 00:07:25,147
I could have just told you that.

232
00:07:25,497 --> 00:07:27,420
And then she told me,

233
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:27,969
honey.

234
00:07:28,149 --> 00:07:30,088
Things I never knew.

235
00:07:30,268 --> 00:07:32,362
That her mother cried in the car

236
00:07:32,362 --> 00:07:34,325
every time they left my house,

237
00:07:34,325 --> 00:07:36,747
because she missed her father so much

238
00:07:36,747 --> 00:07:38,906
and my house reminded her of him.

239
00:07:39,086 --> 00:07:41,229
That Mark had been laid off in

240
00:07:41,229 --> 00:07:43,159
October and hadn't told me,

241
00:07:43,159 --> 00:07:45,945
and that's why he'd called the realtor,

242
00:07:45,945 --> 00:07:48,374
because he was scared and ashamed.

243
00:07:48,554 --> 00:07:50,778
And that Kyle was failing out of

244
00:07:50,778 --> 00:07:53,140
college and wouldn't tell anybody.

245
00:07:53,490 --> 00:07:55,648
I stood at that sink with my

246
00:07:55,648 --> 00:07:57,420
hands in the dishwater,

247
00:07:57,420 --> 00:07:58,577
and I realized,

248
00:07:58,577 --> 00:08:01,583
sweetheart, that I'd only heard half of

249
00:08:01,583 --> 00:08:01,814
it.

250
00:08:01,994 --> 00:08:03,608
Harold was right.

251
00:08:03,788 --> 00:08:06,575
People stop pretending when they think you

252
00:08:06,575 --> 00:08:07,305
can't hear.

253
00:08:07,485 --> 00:08:09,809
But what you hear isn't the whole

254
00:08:09,809 --> 00:08:10,725
truth either.

255
00:08:10,905 --> 00:08:12,135
You hear the fear,

256
00:08:12,135 --> 00:08:13,297
and the meanness,

257
00:08:13,297 --> 00:08:14,527
and the tiredness.

258
00:08:14,707 --> 00:08:16,936
You don't hear the crying in the

259
00:08:16,936 --> 00:08:17,215
car.

260
00:08:17,395 --> 00:08:19,772
It took a nine-year-old to tell me

261
00:08:19,772 --> 00:08:20,402
the rest.

262
00:08:20,752 --> 00:08:22,074
On New Year's Day,

263
00:08:22,074 --> 00:08:24,792
I called everybody back to the house.

264
00:08:24,972 --> 00:08:27,339
And I took out that hearing aid,

265
00:08:27,339 --> 00:08:29,263
and I put it on the table,

266
00:08:29,263 --> 00:08:30,077
and I said,

267
00:08:30,077 --> 00:08:31,926
I owe you all an apology.

268
00:08:32,106 --> 00:08:33,964
I can hear perfectly.

269
00:08:34,144 --> 00:08:37,186
I've heard every word since Thanksgiving.

270
00:08:37,366 --> 00:08:39,809
Fourteen faces went white,

271
00:08:39,809 --> 00:08:40,373
honey.

272
00:08:40,553 --> 00:08:42,840
Denise dropped her coffee cup.

273
00:08:43,020 --> 00:08:45,322
Kyle got up and walked out to

274
00:08:45,322 --> 00:08:45,957
his car.

275
00:08:46,137 --> 00:08:48,633
And Mark put his face in his

276
00:08:48,633 --> 00:08:49,167
hands.

277
00:08:49,518 --> 00:08:50,457
Then I said,

278
00:08:50,457 --> 00:08:52,961
Mark, why didn't you tell me you

279
00:08:52,961 --> 00:08:54,057
lost your job?

280
00:08:54,237 --> 00:08:55,165
And my son,

281
00:08:55,165 --> 00:08:56,514
fifty years old,

282
00:08:56,514 --> 00:08:59,214
started to cry at my dining room

283
00:08:59,214 --> 00:09:01,829
table, the way he cried when he

284
00:09:01,829 --> 00:09:02,503
was six.

285
00:09:02,683 --> 00:09:03,687
He said, Mom,

286
00:09:03,687 --> 00:09:05,772
I didn't want you to worry.

287
00:09:05,952 --> 00:09:08,176
I thought if we sold the house

288
00:09:08,176 --> 00:09:10,178
and got you somewhere safe,

289
00:09:10,178 --> 00:09:12,476
I could help everybody at once.

290
00:09:12,656 --> 00:09:14,591
I didn't mean it the way it

291
00:09:14,591 --> 00:09:15,164
sounded.

292
00:09:15,344 --> 00:09:16,795
I'm so sorry.

293
00:09:17,145 --> 00:09:19,564
And Kyle came back in from the

294
00:09:19,564 --> 00:09:21,579
car after twenty minutes,

295
00:09:21,579 --> 00:09:24,561
red-eyed, and he stood in the doorway

296
00:09:24,561 --> 00:09:26,012
and said, Grandma,

297
00:09:26,012 --> 00:09:26,979
I'm failing.

298
00:09:27,159 --> 00:09:28,982
I'm failing everything,

299
00:09:28,982 --> 00:09:30,171
and I'm scared,

300
00:09:30,171 --> 00:09:32,469
and I didn't know how to tell

301
00:09:32,469 --> 00:09:33,103
anybody.

302
00:09:33,283 --> 00:09:35,408
So I just acted like a jerk.

303
00:09:35,588 --> 00:09:37,347
He was twenty-three,

304
00:09:37,347 --> 00:09:37,875
honey.

305
00:09:38,055 --> 00:09:39,652
And he put his head on my

306
00:09:39,652 --> 00:09:41,695
shoulder like he used to when he

307
00:09:41,695 --> 00:09:43,419
was little, and I held him.

308
00:09:43,768 --> 00:09:46,241
And then my daughter Susan stood up

309
00:09:46,241 --> 00:09:47,230
and said, Mom,

310
00:09:47,230 --> 00:09:49,562
I need to tell you something too.

311
00:09:49,742 --> 00:09:51,975
And she told me about crying in

312
00:09:51,975 --> 00:09:52,551
the car.

313
00:09:52,731 --> 00:09:53,869
Every time.

314
00:09:54,049 --> 00:09:55,408
For four years.

315
00:09:55,588 --> 00:09:58,080
She said, I can't stand being in

316
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:00,027
this house without Daddy,

317
00:10:00,027 --> 00:10:02,208
so I stopped coming as much,

318
00:10:02,208 --> 00:10:03,999
and then I felt guilty,

319
00:10:03,999 --> 00:10:06,491
and the guilt made me short with

320
00:10:06,491 --> 00:10:06,803
you.

321
00:10:06,983 --> 00:10:08,981
And we stood in the middle of

322
00:10:08,981 --> 00:10:11,394
my dining room and held each other,

323
00:10:11,394 --> 00:10:13,461
and cried for Harold together.

324
00:10:13,811 --> 00:10:16,215
Here's what I did with the truth,

325
00:10:16,215 --> 00:10:17,016
sweetheart.

326
00:10:17,196 --> 00:10:19,228
I didn't sell the house.

327
00:10:19,408 --> 00:10:21,936
But I invited Mark and Denise to

328
00:10:21,936 --> 00:10:24,070
move in for a year while he

329
00:10:24,070 --> 00:10:25,255
found new work,

330
00:10:25,255 --> 00:10:26,125
and he did,

331
00:10:26,125 --> 00:10:26,757
by June.

332
00:10:26,937 --> 00:10:28,584
I paid for Kyle to go to

333
00:10:28,584 --> 00:10:31,055
the community college and live in my

334
00:10:31,055 --> 00:10:33,320
spare room, and he helped me with

335
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:33,938
the yard.

336
00:10:34,118 --> 00:10:36,602
He graduated two years later.

337
00:10:36,782 --> 00:10:39,336
He brings me lunch every Tuesday.

338
00:10:39,516 --> 00:10:42,037
And he says my house smells like

339
00:10:42,037 --> 00:10:42,825
Christmas.

340
00:10:43,175 --> 00:10:44,441
And Lily?

341
00:10:44,621 --> 00:10:46,662
Lily is nineteen now,

342
00:10:46,662 --> 00:10:47,245
honey.

343
00:10:47,425 --> 00:10:50,414
She's studying to be an audiologist,

344
00:10:50,414 --> 00:10:51,825
a hearing doctor.

345
00:10:52,005 --> 00:10:54,060
She says it's because of me.

346
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:55,638
And every Christmas,

347
00:10:55,638 --> 00:10:57,735
she gives me the same present.

348
00:10:57,915 --> 00:10:59,908
That broken hearing aid,

349
00:10:59,908 --> 00:11:01,653
wrapped in new paper,

350
00:11:01,653 --> 00:11:04,062
with a little card that says,

351
00:11:04,062 --> 00:11:06,471
Grandma, I'm still listening.

352
00:11:06,651 --> 00:11:08,656
I have ten of those cards in

353
00:11:08,656 --> 00:11:10,088
a box on my dresser.

354
00:11:10,437 --> 00:11:13,044
Here's what I want you to remember,

355
00:11:13,044 --> 00:11:13,491
honey.

356
00:11:13,671 --> 00:11:15,863
If you go looking for what people

357
00:11:15,863 --> 00:11:17,191
really think of you,

358
00:11:17,191 --> 00:11:18,187
you'll find it.

359
00:11:18,367 --> 00:11:19,981
But you'll only find half.

360
00:11:20,161 --> 00:11:21,758
The other half is fear,

361
00:11:21,758 --> 00:11:23,007
and money trouble,

362
00:11:23,007 --> 00:11:25,576
and shame, and love that doesn't know

363
00:11:25,576 --> 00:11:27,104
how to come out right.

364
00:11:27,284 --> 00:11:29,791
Don't stop at the half that hurts.

365
00:11:29,971 --> 00:11:31,121
Find your Lily.

366
00:11:31,301 --> 00:11:33,588
Ask the one who's quiet.

367
00:11:33,768 --> 00:11:35,568
They always know the rest.

368
00:11:35,917 --> 00:11:37,605
Tell me in the comments,

369
00:11:37,605 --> 00:11:38,379
sweetheart.

370
00:11:38,559 --> 00:11:40,544
Was I wrong to pretend?

371
00:11:40,724 --> 00:11:42,744
Be honest with Grandma.

372
00:11:42,924 --> 00:11:45,102
And who's the quiet one in your

373
00:11:45,102 --> 00:11:46,999
family who sees everything?

374
00:11:47,179 --> 00:11:49,607
There are more of my family's stories

375
00:11:49,607 --> 00:11:51,904
in the playlist right there on your

376
00:11:51,904 --> 00:11:53,938
screen, and I'll see you in the

377
00:11:53,938 --> 00:11:54,528
next one.

378
00:11:54,708 --> 00:11:56,450
Good night, honey.
